    shortDescription: "C is a procedural language giving direct, low-level control over memory and data layout, with no built-in support for object-oriented concepts. C++ is largely a superset that adds...",
    explanation: "Interviewers use this to check whether you understand core C++ language rules and can explain why the language behaves differently in common real-world cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the language rule stated accurately, not just a memorized definition.\n- They want you to explain the practical consequence for object design, correctness, or performance.\n- They want a small example that demonstrates the rule without unnecessary complexity.",
    sampleAnswer: "C is a procedural language giving direct, low-level control over memory and data layout, with no built-in support for object-oriented concepts. C++ is largely a superset that adds classes, inheritance, polymorphism, templates, exceptions, RAII, operator overloading, and a large standard library (containers, algorithms, smart pointers), while still retaining C's low-level capability - raw pointers, manual memory management, and compatibility with most C code. This is why C++ is used when a project wants both strong abstraction (for maintainability at scale) and performance close to C.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nC and C++ are closely related programming languages, but they differ mainly in their programming paradigms and level of abstraction. C is primarily a procedural programming language that organizes programs around functions, structures, and explicit data manipulation. It provides low-level control over memory and data layout through pointers, manual memory management, and constructs such as `malloc()` and `free()`.\n\nC++ started as an extension of C and supports procedural programming, but it also provides object-oriented, generic, and other higher-level programming techniques. It adds features such as classes, objects, inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation, templates, function and operator overloading, exceptions, namespaces, RAII, and the C++ Standard Library. C++ also provides modern abstractions such as `std::vector`, `std::string`, smart pointers, and standard algorithms while still allowing low-level operations when necessary.\n\nOne important difference is memory management. In C, developers commonly manage dynamically allocated memory explicitly using functions such as `malloc()`, `calloc()`, `realloc()`, and `free()`. In C++, memory can still be managed manually with `new` and `delete`, but modern C++ generally encourages RAII and smart pointers such as `std::unique_ptr` and `std::shared_ptr` to make resource management safer and reduce memory leaks.\n\nAnother major difference is object-oriented programming. C does not have native classes, inheritance, or polymorphism. Developers can simulate some of these concepts using structures and function pointers. C++ provides these concepts directly through language features such as classes, virtual functions, inheritance, and templates.\n\nC is often preferred for operating-system components, embedded systems, firmware, device drivers, and other environments where a small language, predictable behavior, and direct hardware interaction are important. C++ is commonly used for large systems software, game engines, performance-critical applications, desktop applications, and other projects where both performance and higher-level abstractions are valuable.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// C-style approach\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* numbers = (int*)malloc(10 * sizeof(int));\n\n// Use the allocated memory...\n\nfree(numbers);\n```\n\nIn C, the programmer is responsible for allocating and releasing the memory correctly. Forgetting to call `free()` can result in a memory leak.\n\n```cpp\n// Modern C++ approach\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> numbers(10);\n\n// Use the vector...\n```\n\nHere, `std::vector` manages its own dynamically allocated memory. When the vector goes out of scope, its resources are automatically released. This is an example of RAII, which is one of the important resource-management techniques in modern C++.\n\nFor object-oriented programming, C++ also provides features that C does not natively provide:\n\n```cpp\nclass Employee {\npublic:\n    virtual void work() {\n        // Default implementation\n    }\n};\n\nclass Developer : public Employee {\npublic:\n    void work() override {\n        // Developer-specific implementation\n    }\n};\n```\n\nThe `Developer` class inherits from `Employee` and overrides its `work()` function. This provides built-in inheritance and runtime polymorphism, whereas C would require these patterns to be implemented manually using structures and function pointers.\n\n**In short:** C focuses primarily on procedural programming and direct low-level control, while C++ provides C's low-level capabilities together with powerful abstractions such as object-oriented programming, templates, RAII, and the Standard Library. The choice depends on the project's requirements: C is often valuable when simplicity and low-level control are priorities, while C++ is useful when a system needs both high performance and more sophisticated abstractions at scale.",
