    subcategorySlug: "cpp-fundamentals",
    experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
    difficulty: "EASY",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Const correctness means using const consistently to express which data isn't meant to change - const parameters, const member functions, and const references - so the compiler can...",
    explanation: "Interviewers use this to check whether you understand core C++ language rules and can explain why the language behaves differently in common real-world cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the language rule stated accurately, not just a memorized definition.\n- They want you to explain the practical consequence for object design, correctness, or performance.\n- They want a small example that demonstrates the rule without unnecessary complexity.",
    sampleAnswer: "Const correctness means using const consistently to express which data isn't meant to change - const parameters, const member functions, and const references - so the compiler can catch accidental mutation at compile time rather than it becoming a runtime bug. A const member function promises not to modify the object's observable state (technically, the implicit this pointer becomes a pointer-to-const inside that function), which matters because it's the only way to call a member function on a const object or through a const reference; the compiler enforces this by rejecting non-const member calls or member modification inside a function marked const, except for members explicitly marked mutable.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nConst correctness basically means using `const` to clearly express which parts of a program are not supposed to modify data. It gives the compiler more information about your intentions and lets it catch accidental modifications.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid printName(const string& name) {\n    cout << name;\n}\n```\n\nThe `const` here means the function promises not to modify the string passed to it. Because of that, the function can also accept a const string.\n\nThe same idea applies to member functions. Consider this class:\n\n```cpp\nclass Person {\nprivate:\n    string name;\n\npublic:\n    string getName() const {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    void setName(string newName) {\n        name = newName;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nThe `const` after `getName()` is important. It means that calling `getName()` is not supposed to modify the object.\n\nInside a const member function, the compiler treats the object as const. In other words, the function effectively receives a const version of `this`, so you can't normally modify the object's non-mutable data members.\n\nFor example, this won't compile:\n\n```cpp\nstring getName() const {\n    name = \"Changed\";   // Error\n    return name;\n}\n```\n\nThe compiler rejects it because `getName()` promised that it wouldn't modify the object.\n\nThis becomes especially useful when working with const objects:\n\n```cpp\nconst Person person;\n```\n\nA const object can only call member functions that are themselves marked `const`, because calling a non-const member function could potentially change the object.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    string getName() const {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    void setName(string value) {\n        name = value;\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    string name;\n};\n\nconst Person person;\nperson.getName();   // OK\nperson.setName(\"John\"); // Error\n```\n\nThis is one of the main reasons const member functions matter. They allow read-only objects to use functions that only inspect their state.\n\nConst correctness is not limited to member functions. It also appears with pointers, references, parameters, and return values. For example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid print(const Person& person);\n```\n\nThis tells the caller that the function only needs to read the object and will not modify it.\n\nA common interview point is that `const` is part of the member function's type. Therefore, a class can have both a const and non-const version of a function:\n\n```cpp\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    string& getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    const string& getName() const {\n        return name;\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    string name;\n};\n```\n\nA non-const object gets the non-const version, while a const object gets the const version.\n\nThere is also a `mutable` keyword. A mutable member can be changed even from a const member function. This is useful for things such as caches or counters that don't represent a logical change to the object's externally visible state.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nclass Data {\nprivate:\n    mutable int accessCount = 0;\n\npublic:\n    void read() const {\n        accessCount++;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nHere `read()` is const because it doesn't logically modify the object's data from the caller's point of view, even though it updates the internal counter.\n\nIn short, const correctness is about making the contract of your code clear: if something is marked `const`, it should not unexpectedly modify the data it refers to. A const member function is treated differently because the compiler needs to enforce that promise and prevent it from modifying the object's normal state."
