    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "The primary, guaranteed effect of inline today is exempting a function from the One Definition Rule violation that would otherwise occur if it's defined (not just declared) identic...",
    explanation: "Interviewers use this to check whether you understand core C++ language rules and can explain why the language behaves differently in common real-world cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the language rule stated accurately, not just a memorized definition.\n- They want you to explain the practical consequence for object design, correctness, or performance.\n- They want a small example that demonstrates the rule without unnecessary complexity.",
    sampleAnswer: "The primary, guaranteed effect of inline today is exempting a function from the One Definition Rule violation that would otherwise occur if it's defined (not just declared) identically in multiple translation units - which is exactly why functions defined directly in headers need to be inline (or a template, or constexpr, which implies inline). The common misconception is that inline forces the compiler to substitute the function's body at each call site to avoid call overhead; modern compilers make that inlining decision independently based on their own heuristics and optimization level, regardless of whether the keyword is present, so inline is best understood as a linkage rule, not a performance guarantee.",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nIn modern C++, `inline` is mainly about allowing the same function or variable to be defined in multiple translation units without violating the One Definition Rule. It does **not** mean that the compiler must replace a function call with the function's actual code.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a header file:\n\n```cpp\ninline int square(int x) {\n    return x * x;\n}\n```\n\nThat header can be included by several `.cpp` files. Because the function is marked `inline`, having the same definition appear in multiple translation units is allowed, as long as the definitions are equivalent.\n\nA very common misconception is that `inline` means **\"the compiler will put the function's code directly at the call site.\"** That's not what the keyword guarantees.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\ninline int add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n```\n\nThe compiler may choose to inline the call:\n\n```cpp\nint result = add(10, 20);\n```\n\nso that it effectively behaves like:\n\n```cpp\nint result = 10 + 20;\n```\n\nBut the compiler is free not to do that. Modern compilers make inlining decisions based on optimization settings, function size, call frequency, code generation, and other factors. They can also inline functions that were never marked `inline`.\n\nSo there are really two different ideas that often get confused:\n\n- **`inline` keyword:** primarily affects the One Definition Rule and linkage/definition rules.\n- **Function inlining as an optimization:** replacing a function call with the function's body to potentially reduce call overhead.\n\nFor example, a function defined inside a class definition is implicitly inline:\n\n```cpp\nclass Calculator {\npublic:\n    int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nYou don't need to explicitly write `inline` here.\n\nIt's also worth noting that `inline` can be used with variables in modern C++:\n\n```cpp\ninline int maxConnections = 100;\n```\n\nThis allows the variable to have one shared definition across translation units when placed in a header.\n\nIn short, **don't use `inline` as a command telling the compiler to optimize a function call.** Think of it primarily as a language rule that allows certain definitions to appear in multiple translation units. Whether a function actually gets inlined is ultimately a compiler optimization decision."
,    keyPoints: ["They want the language rule stated accurately, not just a memorized definition.", "They want you to explain the practical consequence for object design, correctness, or performance.", "They want a small example that demonstrates the rule without unnecessary complexity."],
