    question: "What is an lvalue, and how does it differ from an rvalue?",
    slug: "22-what-is-an-lvalue-and-how-does-it-differ-from-an-rvalue",
    categoryName: "Move Semantics and References",
    categorySlug: "cpp-programming/move-semantics-and-references",
    subcategoryName: "Move Semantics and References",
    subcategorySlug: "move-semantics-and-references",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "An lvalue refers to an object that has an identifiable, persistent location in memory and can generally appear on the left side of an assignment (a named variable, a dereferenced p...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand C++ value categories, resource transfer, and the mechanisms behind efficient object passing.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.\n- They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.\n- They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself.",
    sampleAnswer: "An lvalue refers to an object that has an identifiable, persistent location in memory and can generally appear on the left side of an assignment (a named variable, a dereferenced pointer, an element accessed via subscript). An rvalue is typically a temporary value without a persistent, addressable identity beyond the current expression (a literal, the result of most non-reference-returning function calls, an arithmetic expression's result). This distinction is what move semantics is built on: an rvalue reference (T&&) can bind to an rvalue specifically because it's safe to 'steal' resources from something about to be discarded anyway, which wouldn't be safe to do to an lvalue that the rest of the program might still use.",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nAn **lvalue** is an expression that generally refers to an object with an identifiable location in memory, while an **rvalue** is generally a temporary value or expression result that isn't treated as a persistent object you can normally assign to.\n\nA simple example is:\n\n```cpp\nint x = 10;\n```\n\nHere, `x` is an lvalue because it refers to a specific object. The value `10` is an rvalue because it is a temporary value used to initialize `x`.\n\nThis is why this works:\n\n```cpp\nx = 20;\n```\n\n`x` can appear on the left side of an assignment because it is an lvalue.\n\nBut this doesn't make sense:\n\n```cpp\n10 = x;   // Error\n```\n\nbecause `10` is an rvalue and isn't a modifiable object that you can assign to.\n\nAnother common example is:\n\n```cpp\nint a = 10;\nint b = a + 5;\n```\n\nHere:\n\n```text\na       -> lvalue\n5       -> rvalue\n(a + 5) -> rvalue\nb       -> lvalue\n```\n\nThe distinction becomes particularly important with references:\n\n```cpp\nint x = 10;\n\nint& ref = x;       // OK: lvalue reference to lvalue\nint&& rref = 10;    // OK: rvalue reference to rvalue\n```\n\nA normal lvalue reference (`T&`) generally binds to an lvalue:\n\n```cpp\nint& ref = x;\n```\n\nAn rvalue reference (`T&&`) is designed to bind to rvalues:\n\n```cpp\nint&& ref = 10;\n```\n\nThis is one of the foundations of move semantics. C++ can distinguish an object that has a persistent identity from a temporary object whose resources may be safely transferred.\n\nThere's a small terminology detail worth knowing: C++'s full value-category system is more precise than simply saying \"lvalue means left side and rvalue means right side.\" There are also categories such as **xvalue** and **prvalue**, with lvalue and rvalue being broader categories.\n\nFor most interviews, though, the useful mental model is:\n\n```text\nlvalue -> an expression referring to an identifiable object\nrvalue -> a temporary/value expression that can often be moved from\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string name = \"John\";\n```\n\n`name` is an lvalue. The temporary string produced by something like:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string(\"John\")\n```\n\nis an rvalue.\n\nIn short, **lvalues represent objects with an identifiable identity, while rvalues generally represent temporary values or expressions. This distinction is important in C++ because it controls which references can bind to an expression and is a key part of move semantics and overload resolution.**"
,    keyPoints: ["They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.", "They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.", "They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself."],
