    question: "What is a constant reference (const T&), and why is it commonly used for function parameters?",
    slug: "24-what-is-a-constant-reference-const-t-and-why-is-it-commonly-used-for-function-parameters",
    categoryName: "Move Semantics and References",
    categorySlug: "cpp-programming/move-semantics-and-references",
    subcategoryName: "Move Semantics and References",
    subcategorySlug: "move-semantics-and-references",
    experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
    difficulty: "EASY",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A const T& parameter binds to the caller's argument without copying it (unlike a plain by-value T parameter) while preventing the function from modifying it (unlike a plain T& para...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand C++ value categories, resource transfer, and the mechanisms behind efficient object passing.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.\n- They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.\n- They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself.",
    sampleAnswer: "A const T& parameter binds to the caller's argument without copying it (unlike a plain by-value T parameter) while preventing the function from modifying it (unlike a plain T& parameter), which makes it the conventional choice for passing objects that are potentially expensive to copy - strings, vectors, user-defined classes - when the function only needs to read them. An added benefit is that a const reference can bind to a temporary (an rvalue), extending that temporary's lifetime for the duration of the reference, which a plain non-const reference cannot do.",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA `const T&` is a reference to an object that the function can read but cannot modify through that reference. It is commonly used for function parameters because it lets you pass an existing object without making a copy, while also protecting the object from accidental modification.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid printName(const std::string& name) {\n    std::cout << name;\n}\n```\n\nIf we call:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string name = \"John\";\nprintName(name);\n```\n\nthe function receives a reference to the original string. It doesn't need to create another complete copy of the string.\n\nThe `const` part is important because it means the function cannot do this:\n\n```cpp\nvoid printName(const std::string& name) {\n    name = \"Changed\";  // Error\n}\n```\n\nThis makes the function's intent clear: it only needs to read the argument.\n\nThis can matter a lot with large objects. Suppose you have:\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<int> numbers(1000000);\n```\n\nIf you pass it by value:\n\n```cpp\nvoid process(std::vector<int> numbers) {\n}\n```\n\na copy of the vector is created when the function is called.\n\nWith a const reference:\n\n```cpp\nvoid process(const std::vector<int>& numbers) {\n}\n```\n\nthe function can access the original vector without copying all its elements.\n\nAnother useful property is that a const reference can bind to temporary values:\n\n```cpp\nvoid print(const std::string& text) {\n    std::cout << text;\n}\n\nprint(\"Hello\");\n```\n\nThe temporary string created for the call can safely be bound to the const reference for the duration of the call.\n\nCompare the three common forms:\n\n```cpp\nvoid process(Data data);             // Makes a copy\nvoid process(Data& data);            // Can modify the original\nvoid process(const Data& data);      // Reads the original without copying\n```\n\nThere is one important nuance: `const T&` isn't automatically the best choice for every parameter. For small, cheap-to-copy types such as `int`, `double`, or `bool`, passing by value is often simpler and just as efficient:\n\n```cpp\nvoid setAge(int age);\n```\n\nYou also shouldn't automatically use `const T&` for every type. Modern C++ parameter choices depend on size, ownership, lifetime, and whether the function needs to modify or consume the argument.\n\nIn short, **`const T&` is commonly used when a function needs read-only access to an existing object and you want to avoid making a potentially expensive copy. It also clearly communicates that the function isn't supposed to modify the caller's object.**"
,    keyPoints: ["They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.", "They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.", "They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself."],
    commonMistakes: [],
