    question: "Predict the behavior: a function parameter is declared as a pointer to const (const int*) versus a const pointer (int* const), and code attempts to modify the pointer or the pointee in each case.",
    slug: "27-predict-the-behavior-a-function-parameter-is-declared-as-a-pointer-to-const-const-int-versus-a-const",
    categoryName: "Pointers and Coding Problems",
    categorySlug: "cpp-programming/pointers-and-coding-problems",
    subcategoryName: "Pointers and Coding Problems",
    subcategorySlug: "pointers-and-coding-problems",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "const int* p means p points to data that must be treated as const through p - you can reassign p to point elsewhere, but can't modify *p through p. int* const p means p itself is c...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you can reason about addresses, ownership, indirection, and data structures at a low level.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain what is stored and how the operations change the data structure.\n- They want you to identify lifetime, ownership, and safety considerations.\n- They want a working example with correct pointer manipulation and no hidden undefined behavior.",
    sampleAnswer: "const int* p means p points to data that must be treated as const through p - you can reassign p to point elsewhere, but can't modify *p through p. int* const p means p itself is const - it must always point to the same address, but the data it points to can be modified through it (unless that data is separately const). Reading these declarations right-to-left from the const keyword's position is the standard trick: const int* reads as 'pointer to const int,' while int* const reads as 'const pointer to int.'",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe easiest way to remember the difference is to look at what `const` is applying to.\n\nWith:\n\n```cpp\nconst int* ptr;\n```\n\nthe **integer is const through the pointer**, but the pointer itself can be changed.\n\nWith:\n\n```cpp\nint* const ptr;\n```\n\nthe **pointer is const**, but the integer it points to can be changed.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nint a = 10;\nint b = 20;\n\nconst int* ptr = &a;\n```\n\nThis is allowed:\n\n```cpp\nptr = &b;       // OK\n```\n\nbecause the pointer itself isn't const.\n\nBut this isn't allowed:\n\n```cpp\n*ptr = 30;      // Error\n```\n\nbecause `ptr` points to a const `int` through that pointer.\n\nNow look at:\n\n```cpp\nint* const ptr = &a;\n```\n\nThis is allowed:\n\n```cpp\n*ptr = 30;      // OK\n```\n\nbecause the pointed-to `int` isn't const.\n\nBut this isn't allowed:\n\n```cpp\nptr = &b;       // Error\n```\n\nbecause the pointer itself is const.\n\nSo:\n\n```text\nconst int* ptr\n\npointer can change     -> YES\npointee can change     -> NO\n```\n\nand:\n\n```text\nint* const ptr\n\npointer can change     -> NO\npointee can change     -> YES\n```\n\nThere's also a third form:\n\n```cpp\nconst int* const ptr = &a;\n```\n\nNow both are const:\n\n```text\npointer can change     -> NO\npointee can change     -> NO\n```\n\nAs function parameters, this distinction is useful. For example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid print(const int* ptr) {\n    cout << *ptr;\n}\n```\n\nThe function can change where its local pointer points, but it cannot modify the integer through that pointer.\n\nWhereas:\n\n```cpp\nvoid update(int* const ptr) {\n    *ptr = 100;\n}\n```\n\nThe function can't make `ptr` point somewhere else, but it can modify the integer.\n\nOne subtle point is that top-level `const` on a by-value parameter like `int* const ptr` doesn't affect the caller because the pointer itself is copied into the function. It mainly prevents the function from reassigning its local copy of the pointer.\n\nIn short, **`const int*` protects the pointee, while `int* const` protects the pointer itself. `const int* const` protects both.**"
,    keyPoints: ["They want you to explain what is stored and how the operations change the data structure.", "They want you to identify lifetime, ownership, and safety considerations.", "They want a working example with correct pointer manipulation and no hidden undefined behavior."],
