    question: "What is a function template, and how does the compiler generate code from it?",
    slug: "28-what-is-a-function-template-and-how-does-the-compiler-generate-code-from-it",
    categoryName: "Templates and Generic Programming",
    categorySlug: "cpp-programming/templates-and-generic-programming",
    subcategoryName: "Templates and Generic Programming",
    subcategorySlug: "templates-and-generic-programming",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A function template lets you write a single generic definition parameterized by one or more types (or, with C++20, constrained by concepts), and the compiler generates ('instantiat...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to evaluate whether you can reason about compile-time generic programming and type deduction instead of treating templates as simple text substitution.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain when code is instantiated or types are deduced.\n- They want you to distinguish similar mechanisms such as auto, decltype, specialization, and runtime polymorphism.\n- They want a practical example showing why the choice matters in maintainable C++ code.",
    sampleAnswer: "A function template lets you write a single generic definition parameterized by one or more types (or, with C++20, constrained by concepts), and the compiler generates ('instantiates') a concrete function for each distinct set of template arguments actually used in the program, at compile time. This gives the performance of a hand-written type-specific function (no runtime type dispatch overhead) with the reusability of writing the logic once, at the cost of longer compile times and potential code bloat if the same template is instantiated for many distinct types.",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA function template lets you write one generic function that can work with different types. Instead of writing the same logic separately for `int`, `double`, `string`, and so on, you write the logic once and let the compiler create the appropriate version when it is used.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT maximum(T a, T b) {\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n```\n\nNow the same function can be used with different types:\n\n```cpp\nint a = maximum(10, 20);\ndouble b = maximum(3.5, 2.5);\n```\n\nThe compiler looks at the arguments and deduces the template parameter. Conceptually, it can generate something like:\n\n```cpp\nint maximum(int a, int b) {\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n```\n\nand:\n\n```cpp\ndouble maximum(double a, double b) {\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n```\n\nThese aren't literally required to appear as separate source-level functions. This is just a useful way to understand what template instantiation is doing.\n\nThis process is called **template instantiation**. The compiler generates the required specialization when the template is used with a particular set of template arguments.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nmaximum<int>(10, 20);\nmaximum<double>(3.5, 2.5);\n```\n\nexplicitly specifies the type, while normally you can let the compiler deduce it:\n\n```cpp\nmaximum(10, 20);\n```\n\nTemplates are checked based on how they are instantiated. For example:\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT add(T a, T b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n```\n\nThis works for types that support `operator+`.\n\nIf you try to instantiate it with a type that doesn't support the required operation, the compiler will produce an error.\n\nIn modern C++, concepts can make these requirements much clearer:\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nrequires requires(T a, T b) {\n    a + b;\n}\nT add(T a, T b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n```\n\nThe important thing to understand is that a function template isn't simply one runtime function that handles every possible type. The compiler uses the template as a recipe for generating type-specific code at compile time.\n\nIn short, **a function template is a compile-time blueprint for a family of functions. When the program uses the template with a particular type, the compiler instantiates the template for that type and generates code appropriate for it.**"
,    keyPoints: ["They want you to explain when code is instantiated or types are deduced.", "They want you to distinguish similar mechanisms such as auto, decltype, specialization, and runtime polymorphism.", "They want a practical example showing why the choice matters in maintainable C++ code."],
