    shortDescription: "Prefer templates when the set of types is known and fixed at compile time, performance is critical enough that virtual-call overhead and lack of inlining matter, and the types invo...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "Prefer templates when the set of types is known and fixed at compile time, performance is critical enough that virtual-call overhead and lack of inlining matter, and the types involved don't need to share a common base class (duck typing based purely on supported operations, as with STL algorithms operating over any iterator type). Prefer runtime polymorphism when the concrete type genuinely isn't known until runtime (plugins, heterogeneous collections of objects processed uniformly, a factory returning different subclasses based on runtime input), when binary size matters more than raw call overhead (templates can bloat code size with many instantiations), or when the interface needs to be usable across a dynamically-loaded library boundary, where template instantiation visibility becomes awkward.",
        detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe choice between templates and virtual functions usually comes down to **when you need to make the type decision**. Templates give you polymorphism at compile time, while virtual functions give you polymorphism at runtime.\n\nWith templates, the concrete type is known when the code is compiled:\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nvoid process(const T& object) {\n    object.run();\n}\n```\n\nIf we call:\n\n```cpp\nWorker worker;\nprocess(worker);\n```\n\nthe compiler knows that `T` is `Worker` and generates code for that type.\n\nThis is a good fit when the algorithm doesn't really care about a common inheritance hierarchy. It just needs the supplied type to provide certain operations.\n\nFor example, several completely unrelated types could work with the same template:\n\n```cpp\nclass FileLogger {\npublic:\n    void write() {}\n};\n\nclass ConsoleLogger {\npublic:\n    void write() {}\n};\n```\n\nA template can work with both because both provide `write()`:\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nvoid log(T& logger) {\n    logger.write();\n}\n```\n\nThere is no need to create a common base class just for this purpose.\n\nTemplates can also give the compiler more opportunities to optimize. Since the concrete type is known at compile time, the compiler may inline function calls and remove abstraction overhead when optimization allows it.\n\nRuntime polymorphism is a better fit when the actual type needs to remain unknown until runtime.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape {\npublic:\n    double area() const override {\n        return 10.0;\n    }\n};\n\nclass Square : public Shape {\npublic:\n    double area() const override {\n        return 20.0;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nNow we can store different shapes together:\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<std::unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;\n\nshapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Circle>());\nshapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Square>());\n```\n\nThe concrete types can be chosen while the program is running. Code that works with `Shape*` or `Shape&` doesn't need to know whether it received a `Circle` or `Square`.\n\nThis is something templates don't naturally provide. A `std::vector<T>` has one concrete `T` type for that particular instantiation, whereas a polymorphic collection can contain different derived types behind a common interface.\n\nThere are also design considerations beyond performance.\n\nTemplates are often a good choice when:\n\n```text\n- The behavior is naturally generic.\n- The types don't need a common base class.\n- The concrete type is known at compile time.\n- You want compile-time checking of required operations.\n- You want opportunities for aggressive optimization.\n```\n\nVirtual functions are often a better choice when:\n\n```text\n- Different concrete objects need to be handled through one interface.\n- The concrete type is selected at runtime.\n- You need heterogeneous collections.\n- You want to add implementations without changing code that uses the interface.\n- Runtime substitution is part of the design.\n```\n\nFor example, imagine a payment system where the payment method comes from a configuration file:\n\n```text\nCreditCard\nPayPal\nBankTransfer\n```\n\nIf the program decides which implementation to use at runtime, a polymorphic interface can make sense:\n\n```cpp\nstd::unique_ptr<PaymentProcessor> processor;\n```\n\nOn the other hand, if you're writing a generic sorting or mathematical algorithm that should work with many types known at compile time, templates are usually a much better fit.\n\nIt's also possible to combine both approaches. A system may use templates internally for efficient generic code while exposing a runtime-polymorphic interface at a higher architectural level.\n\nOne common mistake is choosing templates simply because they're \"faster\" or virtual functions simply because they're \"more object-oriented.\" The real question is what kind of variability the design needs.\n\nIn short, **prefer templates when the variation is known at compile time and the code is naturally generic. Prefer virtual functions when different concrete implementations need to be substituted and selected at runtime through a common interface. The decision is primarily about the design and lifetime of the type information, not just raw performance.**"
,    keyPoints: ["They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.", "They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.", "They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case."],
